This program demonstrates **function overloading** by calculating the area of different shapes using the same function name `calculate()`.

The `Area` class contains two overloaded `calculate()` functions. The first function accepts one argument, `side`, and calculates the area of a square. The second function accepts two arguments, `length` and `breadth`, and calculates the area of a rectangle.

In the `main()` function, an object `a` of the `Area` class is created. The statement `a.calculate(5)` calls the square version, while `a.calculate(5, 10)` calls the rectangle version.

The compiler selects the appropriate function based on the number of arguments supplied.

**Output:**

```text
Square Area: 25
Rectangle Area: 50
```

Thus, the program demonstrates how function overloading can be used to calculate different areas using the same function name.
